      <p>Mobile data in Sri Lanka is cheap, fast, and widely available — and having a local SIM card is one of the most practical things you can do on arrival. Data-heavy travellers typically burn through 10–15 GB in two weeks between Google Maps navigation, accommodation searches, and uploading photos. A local SIM card covering all of that costs around USD 5–8. Here's everything you need to know.</p>

      <h2>The Three Networks: Dialog vs. Airtel vs. Mobitel</h2>
      <p>Sri Lanka has three main carriers. <strong>Dialog Axiata</strong> is the largest network and offers the best 4G coverage across the country — particularly in the Hill Country, national parks, and rural areas where the other networks can drop to 3G or lose signal entirely. If you're visiting Ella, Nuwara Eliya, Knuckles or Horton Plains, Dialog is the clear choice. <strong>Airtel Sri Lanka</strong> is strong in urban areas (Colombo, Kandy, Galle) and competitive on pricing, but coverage deteriorates in remote highland areas. <strong>Mobitel</strong> (SLT-Mobitel) is state-owned, with good urban coverage and the strongest presence in the north — useful if you're heading to Jaffna. For most tourist routes — the cultural triangle, hill country, south coast — Dialog wins on reliability.</p>

      <h2>Where to Buy a SIM Card</h2>
      <p>The easiest place is <strong>Bandaranaike International Airport (BIA)</strong> on arrival — all three carriers have counters in the arrivals hall, immediately after customs and before the exit. You can't miss them. Airport SIM prices are slightly higher than town prices but the convenience is worth it: you walk out of the airport with data working. The alternative is buying in town — every supermarket, phone shop, and even many convenience stores sell SIM cards and top-up credits. In Colombo, dedicated carrier stores (Majestic City mall, Liberty Plaza) have the best deal options. In smaller towns, the local phone shop will stock at least Dialog and Airtel.</p>

      <h2>Registration: What You Need</h2>
      <p>All SIM cards in Sri Lanka require registration at the point of purchase — you cannot activate a SIM without showing your passport. The vendor will scan or photograph your passport photo page and complete a brief registration form. This is a legal requirement and is handled entirely at the counter; it takes 2–3 minutes. Your SIM is activated immediately after registration. Keep your passport handy in your carry-on bag rather than your checked luggage for this reason.</p>

      <h2>Costs and Data Packages</h2>
      <p>Prices vary slightly by retailer and change occasionally, but as a guide for <strong>Dialog Axiata tourist packages</strong>: a starter SIM costs around LKR 200–400 (USD 0.60–1.20), and tourist data packages range from 5 GB (LKR 500–700) to 20 GB (LKR 1,200–1,800) with 30-day validity. Calls within Sri Lanka are cheap — local calls are LKR 2–4 per minute. International calls are expensive on a local SIM; use WhatsApp or FaceTime over data instead. Top-up credit (reloading) is available everywhere — look for "Dialog Reload" signs in any shop. You can also top up via the Dialog MyAccount app.</p>

