      <p>The trade-off with travel eSIMs versus local SIMs: a local Dialog SIM will be cheaper for a solo Sri Lanka trip (especially for heavy data users), and local SIMs work on the full Dialog network. Travel eSIMs are more convenient and better value if you're multi-country or prioritise not dealing with airport counters.</p>

      <h2>Tips for Staying Connected</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Download offline maps before leaving cities.</strong> Google Maps allows you to download entire regions for offline use — do this for Sri Lanka over hotel or guesthouse WiFi, not on your data plan.</li>
        <li><strong>Turn off auto-updates and video streaming on mobile data</strong> — apps like Netflix and YouTube will drain a 5 GB package within days if left to auto-play.</li>
        <li><strong>Pocket WiFi routers</strong> are available for hire in Colombo (roughly USD 5–8/day) — useful for groups of 2–4 sharing data, but a hassle to carry and charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Check eSIM compatibility first.</strong> Go to Settings → Mobile Data (iPhone) or Settings → Network → SIM (Android) to confirm your device supports eSIM before purchasing one.</li>
        <li><strong>The train from Kandy to Ella</strong> passes through several mountain tunnels — signal drops briefly but returns. It's not a connectivity dead zone; you can use your phone on most of the journey.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt><strong>Can I use my home SIM card in Sri Lanka?</strong></dt>
        <dd>Yes — most international plans work, but roaming charges are typically very high (USD 5–15/day for data). Buying a local SIM or travel eSIM is almost always significantly cheaper for trips longer than 2–3 days.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Is there 5G in Sri Lanka?</strong></dt>
        <dd>5G is being trialled in Colombo but is not widely available. 4G LTE is the standard in cities and major tourist areas; 3G in rural areas on most networks.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Can I keep my number and port it at home?</strong></dt>
        <dd>Sri Lankan tourist SIMs are prepaid local numbers. You cannot port them internationally. Simply let the SIM expire after your trip.</dd>
        <dt><strong>Does the airport Dialog counter accept credit cards?</strong></dt>
        <dd>Yes — Dialog at BIA airport accepts Visa and Mastercard, as well as cash (LKR or USD).</dd>
      </dl>
